  "family": "Viburnaceae",
  "memo": "&lt;em&gt;Sambucus nigra&lt;/em&gt;, commonly known as elderberry, is a fast-growing deciduous shrub or small tree native to North America and Europe. This species commonly grows in moist, wet habitats with good drainage — such as streambanks, floodplains, and riparian corridors — and is used in landscapes for its ornamental clusters of white flowers and dark purple berries. &lt;em&gt;S. nigra&lt;/em&gt; is a widely distributed species with numerous subspecies and cultivars that are often very similar in appearance and can be difficult to distinguish from one another. Elderberry serves as the sole host plant for the endangered Valley Elderberry Longhorn Beetle (&lt;em&gt;Desmocerus californicus dimorphus&lt;/em&gt;), making it an important species for habitat conservation efforts.",
